  "query_preview": "..\nSunday night through Tuesday: An upper-level ridge will build into \nthe Pacific Northwest behind the exiting trough and ahead of the \nnext incoming weather system. Temperatures will trend warmer early \nin the week, with highs climbing into the upper 70s to upper 80s on \nMonday. Further warming into the 80s is expected Tuesday as an upper-\nlevel trough digs offshore of northern California and Oregon, \namplifying the ridge across the northern Rockies with deep southerly \nflow across the Inland Northwest. Relative humidity values will \ntrend lower, driving a slight rebound in fire danger, though the \nrisk for new fires remains low.\n\nTuesday evening through Thursday: Model guidance remains in good \nagreement that the offshore upper-level trough will take on a \nnegative tilt as it moves inland, though discrepancies persist \nregarding track and timing. A faster, more northerly track would \nbring the strongest synoptic dynamics through the region Tuesday \nevening into early Wednesday morning, introducing shower and \nthunderstorm chances as early as Tuesday night. A slower, more \nsoutherly track would focus most of the dynamics over north Idaho \nand delay precipitation onset until later Wednesday morning or \nafternoon.\n\nPrecipitable water values will climb above 130% of normal, favoring \nwet thunderstorms. However, lightning strikes outside of the main \nrain cores will result in risk for new fire starts along with gusty \noutflow winds. South-southwest wind gusts of 30-40 mph are expected \non Wednesday across the western Columbia Basin and Okanogan Valley. \nSince blowing dust was observed on Saturday (Aug 29) with similar \nwind gusts we move more into the winter wheat planting season, \nreduced visibility from blowing dust will be a concern in areas that \nmiss out on rain in the Columbia Basin and Waterville Plateau.\nA leading shortwave accompanied by an anomalous moisture plume, \nperhaps a weak atmospheric river, will shift inland Wednesday night \ninto Thursday. Ensemble consensus is strong regarding the presence \nof this system, though differences remain on the timing of the \ninitial shortwave and the placement of the trailing low as it digs \nsouthward. This moisture plume brings a 50% or greater chance of \n0.25 inches of rain across the Cascades, far eastern Washington, and \nthe Idaho Panhandle. Probabilities for at least 0.10 inches drop to \n40-50% in the immediate lee of the Cascades and central Columbia \nBasin.\n\nFriday through Sunday: Unsettled weather continues Friday into \nSaturday, though guidance diverges significantly on the evolution of \nthe upper-level low. 59% of ensemble members move the low slowly \ninland across the Pacific Northwest, keeping temperatures cool with \ncontinued precipitation chances. The remaining 41% of ensembles \nretrograde the low offshore, allowing upper-level ridging to rebuild \nSaturday into Sunday with a warming trend for the weekend. /vmt",
